# Trivy ignore list for localhost/rdp-jupyter:dev (Task 14, supply-chain scan).
#
# Policy: we PREFER fixing a finding (upgrade the package, or remove the file)
# over ignoring it. An entry lands here only when it cannot be fixed, and every
# line names the package and why. We never blanket-ignore by severity, wildcard
# or secret rule id. Each entry was reviewed on 2026-11-04 against trivy DB of
# that date; revisit when a Debian fix ships or a core pin is re-evaluated.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Language packages: no fix reachable via upgrade
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# jupyterlab 4.6.3 -> 4.6.4/4.5.11 (XSS via malicious clipboard cell pasting).
# jupyterlab is a CORE pin: requirements.in locks it to the exact version that
# JupyterLab Desktop 4.6.3-1 bundles (conda-meta/jupyterlab-4.6.3), so the venv
# server matches the desktop app, and the vulnerable frontend ships inside the
# Electron asar bundle (bumping only the pip package would not remediate the UI).
# DECISION 2026-11-04 (maintainer): ACCEPT the risk and keep this entry, because
# no patched JupyterLab Desktop release exists yet (core jupyterlab 4.6.4/4.6.5
# are out, but the latest Desktop tag is still 4.6.3-1; the bundle bump is an
# unreleased, merged PR). The deployment is a single-user-per-pod kiosk where the
# only principal is the analyst, so a clipboard/language-pack XSS against oneself
# has low practical value. REVISIT when JupyterLab Desktop publishes 4.6.4-1 or
# later: at that point bump the Desktop bundle and the pip pin together and drop
# this line. See docs/ops/2026-11-04-accepted-risks.md.
CVE-2026-102831  # jupyterlab 4.6.3 XSS: no patched JupyterLab Desktop release; accepted 2026-11-04, revisit on JLD 4.6.4-1+

# setuptools 70.3.0 -> 78.1.1 (path traversal in setuptools.package_index).
# No standalone setuptools is installed: trivy reads this from pip's vendored
# pkg_resources (pip/_vendor/vendor.txt). pip is already at the latest (26.3)
# and still vendors setuptools 70.3.0, so there is no upgrade that fixes it.
# The vulnerable code (PackageIndex download) is not part of pip's vendored
# pkg_resources, so it is not reachable in our usage.
CVE-2025-47273   # setuptools (pip-vendored pkg_resources): pip already latest; vulnerable PackageIndex not present/reachable

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Debian 13 (trixie) base-OS packages: no upstream fix available
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every CVE below has Debian status "affected", "fix_deferred" or
# "will_not_fix" with no fixed version published, so apt cannot upgrade them.
# The OS layer is frozen (Task 9 force-purged Mesa/LLVM and left apt deps
# intentionally broken), so adding/removing OS packages is out of scope.

